04467010498046875, 0.0234375, 0.0084991455078125, 0.00738525390625, 0.00479888916015625, 0.0249481201171875, -0.010589599609375, -0.0225677490234375, 0.0028438568115234375, -0.021240234375, -0.007663726806640625, -0.005359649658203125, 0.0189056396484375, -0.00408935546875, 0.0159912109375, -0.02008056640625, 0.0169219970703125, 0.0261077880859375, 0.025177001953125, -0.005977630615234375, -0.019500732421875, 0.002899169921875, -0.0103912353515625, 0.0228271484375, -0.0192413330078125, 0.0017032623291015625, -0.00493621826171875, -0.036895751953125, 0.00463104248046875, -0.00902557373046875, -0.0002837181091308594, -0.0009775161743164062, 0.0260772705078125, 0.01409912109375, 0.004894256591796875, -0.022979736328125, -0.01099395751953125, 0.0118408203125, 0.010650634765625, 0.00714874267578125, -0.00826263427734375, -0.01357269287109375, 0.0035839080810546875, -0.0143890380859375, -0.00324249267578125, -0.00146484375, -0.02227783203125, 0.020263671875, -0.049285888671875, 0.015472412109375, -0.0236053466796875, -0.024658203125, 0.0117950439453125, -0.01526641845703125, 0.001216888427734375, -0.0019702911376953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Introduction and Background**\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents\u2014autonomous systems that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank of past experiences, solutions, and feedback\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. Unlike traditional reinforcement learning agents, which typically rely on parameter updates or episodic experience replay buffers, memory-based self-improving agents explicitly store and retrieve textual records of their own problem-solving attempts and feedback, enabling them to reason over and reuse past knowledge in a more interpretable way. For example, such agents may solve programming puzzles, storing both their code attempts and the feedback received, and then use this memory to inform future problem-solving.\n\n**Representative Methods**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: (1) **Episodic Memory Replay**, where the agent reuses past solutions and feedback verbatim, and (2) **Memory-Augmented Policy Learning**, where the agent retrieves and integrates relevant memory entries into its decision-making process. In practice, Episodic Memory Replay might involve directly copying a previously successful code snippet when faced with a similar task\u2014essentially treating memory as a lookup table for exact matches. In contrast, Memory-Augmented Policy Learning retrieves several related past attempts (not necessarily exact matches) and synthesizes their lessons, using them as contextual input to guide new actions, such as by conditioning a policy network on retrieved memory. These methods differ in how they leverage stored information\u2014either by direct reuse or by contextual integration, with the latter allowing for more flexible generalization to novel tasks.\n\n**Evaluation Axes and Rationale**\n\nTo systematically assess these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple independent runs (typically 10 per experiment) to quantify variance, which captures the impact of stochasticity in environments and agent decision-making, and (2) randomly shuffling the task order for each run to investigate the effect of task sequence, reflecting the unpredictability of task sequences in real-world deployment scenarios. For each configuration, we report average success rates, standard deviations, and 95% confidence intervals across runs. These axes address gaps in prior work, which often report only single-run results on fixed task orders, potentially masking instability and overestimating generality. In practical applications, agents often face non-deterministic environments and unpredictable task sequences, making it essential to assess both the stability of learning outcomes and the generality of improvement across different conditions.\n\n**Experimental Results**\n\nThrough these experiments, we make two observations that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation exhibits substantial noise in complex environments and on multi-step tasks, with standard deviations in success rates reaching up to 20% across 10 independent runs on a suite of 50 programming puzzles. For example, average final success rates for Memory-Augmented Policy Learning ranged from 60% to 80% depending on the run, with some runs solving all tasks and others failing early and never recovering. For context, baseline agents without memory augmentation typically achieve 50\u201355% success rates under the same conditions, highlighting both the potential and the instability of memory-based approaches. This noise stems from both environmental randomness (e.g., stochastic task feedback) and the agent\u2019s own stochastic policies. (A stochastic policy is one that makes decisions probabilistically rather than deterministically.) Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, leading to inconsistent outcomes across runs. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. For example, prior works often adopt default orderings where simpler tasks precede harder ones, effectively creating an implicit curriculum; this acts as a hidden prerequisite for success, as reversing or randomizing the order can significantly degrade performance, sometimes reducing final success rates by half (e.g., from 80% to 40%).\n\n**Manual Memory Analysis**\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory by reviewing the textual entries stored after each task. Specifically, two annotators independently reviewed a random sample of 100 memory entries (50 per method) and compared their assessments, achieving an inter-annotator agreement of 92%. Our criteria for assessing \"completeness and accuracy\" include: (a) whether all key solution steps are recorded, (b) whether error messages and feedback are transcribed verbatim, (c) whether success criteria are explicitly noted, and (d) whether any omissions or misrepresentations occur. We focus on whether the memory entries provide sufficient detail for another agent (or human) to reconstruct the solution process and learn from past mistakes. Disagreements were resolved through discussion, and results were averaged across annotators.\n\n**Underspecification and Additional Information**\n\nBuilding on the manual analysis, we hypothesize that task and environment underspecification\u2014where agents lack sufficient detail about task requirements or environmental feedback\u2014contributes to this fragility. To validate this hypothesis, we operationalize additional information by providing detailed rubrics (explicit lists of success criteria, stepwise checklists, and example solutions) and richer environment feedback (e.g., explicit error messages, stepwise hints, and clarifying annotations) alongside the agent\u2019s own notes. We then measure the resulting change in task success rates and memory quality. Here, \"memory quality\" is quantified by the proportion of memory entries meeting all completeness and accuracy criteria as assessed by annotators. While this added information partially closes the performance degradation observed in previous experiments (e.g., improving average success rates by 10\u201315% and increasing memory quality scores from 70% to 85%), significant gaps still remain: for example, agents may still misinterpret ambiguous feedback, fail to retrieve relevant memory entries, or overfit to spurious correlations in the data (i.e., learn patterns that do not generalize beyond the training set), leading to persistent failures on certain task types.\n\n**Recommendations and Limitations**\n\nIn summary, each analysis step builds on the previous findings: observed instability motivates manual memory analysis, which in turn motivates interventions to address underspecification. Looking ahead, our work advocates for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents by reporting results across multiple runs and stress-testing them under challenging conditions, such as randomized task orders and noisy environments. We recommend adopting metrics such as variance in success rates, minimum/maximum performance across runs, and robustness to task order as standard benchmarks for reliability. However, implementing these protocols may require increased computational resources and careful experimental design to ensure fair comparisons. Additional confounding factors\u2014such as agent architecture, diversity and difficulty of tasks, and the nature of the memory retrieval mechanism\u2014may also affect generalizability and should be considered in future work. Moreover, our findings on underspecification call for systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight, such as interactive dashboards for memory inspection, tools for injecting clarifying feedback, or mechanisms for human-in-the-loop correction, as explored in recent work on human-AI collaboration (e.g., the OpenAI Evals framework or interactive annotation tools). Challenges in deploying such oversight tools include scalability, user burden, and ensuring that human interventions do not inadvertently bias agent learning. These directions are crucial for preventing agents from failing in unforeseeable ways and for building more reliable self-improving systems."}}